    {
      "id": "frag-4",
      "focal_uid": 4,
      "title": "Central banks keep a bid under the market",
      "insight": "Official-sector buying is where several forces come to rest: de-dollarization and geopolitical risk both drive central-bank purchases, which land directly on the price. It behaves like a demand sink \u2014 a lot feeds in, and it converts all of it into steady, price-supportive buying. This is the 'someone is always bidding' floor under gold.",
      "role": "sink",
      "importance": 100,
      "aci": 1.0,
